(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-anatomy-master-exam-form-f"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form F","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The pituitary gland measures approximately how much in transverse diameter?","choices":["20 mm","6 mm","30 mm","12 mm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pituitary gland is described as a reddish-grey, ovoid body about 12 mm in transverse diameter. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The pituitary gland measures approximately how much in anteroposterior diameter?","choices":["12 mm","8 mm","16 mm","4 mm"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pituitary gland measures about 8 mm in anteroposterior diameter, smaller than its 12 mm transverse diameter. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The pituitary gland weighs approximately how much?","choices":["250 mg","50 mg","500 mg","1000 mg"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pituitary gland weighs approximately 500 mg. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Inferiorly and anteriorly, the pituitary gland is related to which air-filled space?","choices":["Mastoid air cells","Sphenoid air sinus","Ethmoid air cells","Frontal air sinus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Inferiorly and anteriorly, the pituitary gland is related to the sphenoid air sinus. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is related behind (posteriorly) to which bony structure?","choices":["Crista galli","Clivus","Dorsum sellae","Foramen magnum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pituitary gland is related behind to the dorsum sellae. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The diaphragma sellae, which covers the pituitary gland superiorly, is composed of which tissue?","choices":["Dura mater","Arachnoid mater","Pia mater","Periosteum only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The diaphragma sellae is a fold of dura mater covering the pituitary gland superiorly, pierced centrally by the infundibulum. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In the pars distalis, chromophobe cells make up approximately what fraction of the cells and characteristically lack cytoplasmic granules?","choices":["25%","75%","50%","10%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chromophobes make up about 50% of pars distalis cells and lack granules in their cytoplasm. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"In the pars distalis, chromophil cells (which contain cytoplasmic granules and are larger than chromophobes) make up approximately what fraction of the cells?","choices":["10%","50%","90%","25%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Chromophils, which contain granules, make up about 50% of pars distalis cells (split into acidophils and basophils). — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Basophils of the pars distalis, which have bluish cytoplasm, make up approximately what percentage of anterior pituitary cells?","choices":["40%","70%","10%","50%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Basophils make up about 40% of the cells of the pars distalis and secrete TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Compared to acidophils, basophils of the anterior pituitary are generally described as:","choices":["Found only in the posterior lobe","Smaller and lighter staining","Identical in size and staining","Larger and stain deeper"],"answer":3,"exp":"Basophils are generally larger in size and stain more deeply than acidophils. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How many deciduous ('milk') teeth erupt between about 6 and 30 months after birth?","choices":["20","24","32","16"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are 20 deciduous teeth, which erupt between roughly 6 and 30 months of age before eventually being lost and replaced. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"The 20 deciduous teeth are eventually lost and replaced by how many permanent teeth?","choices":["28","36","20","32"],"answer":3,"exp":"The permanent dentition consists of 32 teeth, replacing the 20 deciduous teeth and adding additional posterior teeth including the molars. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which teeth are the last to erupt, often in the late teens or early 20s, and may become impacted if the jaw lacks space?","choices":["The central incisor teeth","The permanent canine teeth","Third molars (wisdom teeth)","The second premolar teeth"],"answer":2,"exp":"The third molars ('wisdom teeth') are the last teeth to erupt and are the most commonly impacted when jaw space is insufficient. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"An impacted tooth is one that:","choices":["Lacks a complete enamel covering over the crown","Erupts significantly earlier than the normal eruption schedule","Cannot erupt properly because of the angle of its growth","Has an unusually short root and stunted development"],"answer":2,"exp":"Impacted teeth cannot erupt properly because of the angle at which they are growing, often emerging only partially or not at all. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of total saliva do the parotid glands, the largest salivary glands, produce?","choices":["60–70%","3–5%","25–30%","90–95%"],"answer":2,"exp":"The parotid glands, though the largest salivary glands, contribute about 25–30% of total saliva. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The sublingual glands, located inferior to the tongue, contribute approximately what share of total saliva?","choices":["50%","60–70%","25–30%","3–5%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sublingual glands contribute only about 3–5% of total saliva, the smallest share of the three paired salivary glands. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"The parotid gland has a close anatomical relationship with which nerve as it courses through the gland?","choices":["Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The parotid gland has a close relationship with the facial nerve, which passes through the substance of the gland. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The parotid duct, which conveys saliva from the parotid gland to the oral cavity, is also known by which eponym?","choices":["Rivinus' duct","Stensen's duct","Wharton's duct","Bartholin's duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The parotid duct opens into the oral cavity by way of Stensen's duct. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The submandibular duct opens into the floor of the mouth through a papilla located:","choices":["Posterior to the sublingual ducts","Lateral to the lingual frenulum","Within the vestibule of the mouth","Directly beneath the hard palate"],"answer":1,"exp":"The submandibular duct opens through a papilla in the floor of the mouth, lateral to the lingual frenulum. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"The dorsal (superior) surface of the tongue is covered with numerous small projections called:","choices":["Rugae","Papillae","Haustra","Plicae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Numerous small projections called papillae cover the superior (dorsal) surface of the tongue. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"The perineum is bounded anteriorly by the pubic symphysis, laterally by the ischial tuberosities, and posteriorly by which structure?","choices":["The ischial spines","The coccyx","The pubic arch","The sacrum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The perineum is circumscribed posteriorly by the coccyx. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The two triangular subdivisions of the perineum are separated by an imaginary horizontal line extending between which structures?","choices":["The ischial spines","The pubic tubercles","The anterior superior iliac spines","The ischial tuberosities"],"answer":3,"exp":"An imaginary horizontal line between the ischial tuberosities separates the perineum into the anterior (urogenital) and posterior (anal) triangles. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In females, the anterior (urogenital) triangle of the perineum contains which structures?","choices":["The anus only","The coccyx","The urethral and vaginal orifices","The base of the penis and scrotum"],"answer":2,"exp":"In females, the urogenital triangle contains the urethral and vaginal orifices. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The boundaries of the female perineum include the mons pubis anteriorly, the medial thighs laterally, and which structures posteriorly?","choices":["The labia majora","The ischial tuberosities","The gluteal folds","The pubic symphysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The gluteal folds, at the superior end of the intergluteal cleft where the coccyx is palpable, form the posterior boundary of the perineum. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"The pelvic diaphragm is formed by the levator ani and which other muscle?","choices":["Obturator internus","Coccygeus","Piriformis","Iliopsoas"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm is formed by the levator ani and coccygeus muscles. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The levator ani muscle is made up of the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and which third component?","choices":["Pubovaginalis","Ischiococcygeus","Iliococcygeus","Bulbospongiosus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The levator ani is composed of the puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which of the following is a stated function of the pelvic diaphragm?","choices":["Supporting pelvic organs and sealing the inferior pelvic opening","Producing cervical mucus that varies with the cycle","Producing estrogen and progesterone continuously","Forming the broad ligament that suspends the uterus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm supports the pelvic organs, seals the inferior opening of the bony pelvis, and lifts to help release feces during defecation. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The anterior peritoneal recess between the uterus and urinary bladder is called the:","choices":["Rectouterine pouch","Paravesical fossa","Pararectal fossa","Vesicouterine pouch"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vesicouterine pouch is the anterior recess between the uterus and the urinary bladder. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"The posterior peritoneal recess between the uterus and the rectum is called the:","choices":["Rectouterine pouch","Vesicouterine pouch","Broad ligament","Ovarian fossa"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rectouterine pouch is the posterior recess between the uterus anteriorly and the rectum posteriorly. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which double fold of peritoneum attaches the ovaries to the broad ligament?","choices":["Mesometrium","Round ligament","Mesosalpinx","Mesovarium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The mesovarium is the double fold of peritoneum attaching the ovaries to the broad ligament. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"According to the lecture, an adult testicle is approximately how long?","choices":["8 cm","5 cm","12 cm","2 cm"],"answer":1,"exp":"The testicle is described as approximately 5 cm long. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"According to the lecture, an adult testicle is approximately how wide?","choices":["5 cm","7 cm","1 cm","3 cm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The testicle is described as approximately 3 cm wide. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"According to the lecture, an adult testicle is approximately how thick?","choices":["4 cm","1 cm","6 cm","2.5 cm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The testicle is described as approximately 2.5 cm thick. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"According to the lecture, an adult testicle weighs approximately how much?","choices":["25–30 g","10–15 g","40–50 g","2–5 g"],"answer":1,"exp":"The testicle is described as weighing approximately 10–15 grams. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"During fetal development, the testes initially form inside the abdominal cavity near which organ?","choices":["The spleen","The kidneys","The bladder","The liver"],"answer":1,"exp":"During development, the testes form inside the abdominal cavity near the kidneys. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"At the '2 months' stage of testicular descent shown in the lecture's diagram, which structures are labeled alongside the developing gonads?","choices":["Diaphragmatic ligament, mesonephric duct, developing kidneys","Epididymis, scrotum, and mature spermatic cord structures","Corpus cavernosum, corpus spongiosum, and the glans penis","Ductus deferens, prostate, and bulbourethral gland ducts"],"answer":0,"exp":"At the 2-month stage, the diagram labels the diaphragmatic ligament, mesonephric duct, and developing kidneys alongside the gonads and gubernaculum testis. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"By the '7 months' stage of testicular descent, the scrotal cavity is shown lined by which structure?","choices":["Tunica albuginea","Dartos fascia","Tunica vaginalis","Cremasteric fascia"],"answer":2,"exp":"At the 7-month stage, the opened scrotal cavity is shown lined by the tunica vaginalis. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"The spermatic cord passes through which structure connecting the scrotal chambers to the peritoneal cavity?","choices":["The inguinal canal","The obturator canal","The femoral canal","The sacral hiatus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The spermatic cord passes through the narrow inguinal canal, which connects the scrotal chambers to the peritoneal cavity. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Within the spermatic cord, the deferential artery specifically supplies which structure?","choices":["The epididymal duct","The scrotal skin layer","The ductus deferens","The testis parenchyma"],"answer":2,"exp":"The deferential artery, one of the named vessels of the spermatic cord, supplies the ductus deferens. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Layers of the scrotum, from superficial to deep, include scrotal skin, superficial scrotal fascia, the dartos muscle, and which additional layer?","choices":["Tunica vaginalis (parietal layer)","Cremaster within cremasteric fascia","Tunica albuginea of the testis","Rete testis network channel"],"answer":1,"exp":"The layers of the scrotum include scrotal skin, superficial scrotal fascia, the dartos muscle, and the cremaster within cremasteric fascia. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"The right kidney typically spans approximately which vertebral levels?","choices":["T10 to L1","T8 to T11","T12 to L3","L2 to L5"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right kidney typically spans approximately T12 to L3. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"The left kidney typically spans approximately which vertebral levels?","choices":["T12 to L3","L1 to L4","T10/11 to L2","T8 to T11"],"answer":2,"exp":"The left kidney typically spans approximately T10/11 to L2, slightly more superior than the right kidney. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"The average length of an adult kidney is approximately:","choices":["6 cm","16 cm","11 cm","20 cm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The average adult kidney is approximately 11 cm in length. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"On which side does the renal vein additionally receive the gonadal vein before draining to the IVC?","choices":["Right side only","Left side only","Both sides equally","Neither side"],"answer":1,"exp":"On the left side, the renal vein additionally receives the gonadal vein before draining toward the IVC. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"According to the lecture, each kidney typically contains approximately how many renal pyramids?","choices":["20 to 25","30 to 40","2 to 4","8 to 15"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each kidney typically contains approximately 8 to 15 renal pyramids. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The apex of each renal pyramid, which points toward the renal sinus, is called the:","choices":["Renal papilla","Renal column","Renal arch","Renal hilum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The apex of each renal pyramid is called the renal papilla. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The base of each renal pyramid lies against which structure?","choices":["The renal cortex","The renal capsule directly","The renal pelvis","The renal sinus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The base of each renal pyramid lies against the renal cortex. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which layer surrounding the kidney is described as completely surrounding it and providing cushioning and insulation?","choices":["Paranephric fat outside the renal fascia","Fibrous (renal) capsule on the cortex","Perinephric fat (adipose capsule)","Renal fascia anchoring the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perinephric fat (adipose capsule) completely surrounds the kidney, providing cushioning and insulation. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which layer surrounding the kidney anchors it to the posterior abdominal wall and peritoneum?","choices":["Perinephric fat","Paranephric fat","Renal fascia","Fibrous (renal) capsule"],"answer":2,"exp":"The renal fascia is dense irregular connective tissue that anchors the kidney to the posterior wall and peritoneum. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Paranephric (pararenal) fat is located between which two structures?","choices":["The renal sinus and renal pelvis","The renal capsule and perinephric fat","The renal fascia and peritoneum","The cortex and medulla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Paranephric fat lies between the renal fascia and the peritoneum, external to the other kidney-surrounding layers. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
